{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Vallea is a genus of trees in the family Elaeocarpaceae.","title":"Vallea"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Trees in this genus are native to the Andes mountain range in South America and are classified in two species:","title":"Vallea"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Zamia is a genus of cycad of the family Zamiaceae, native to Mexico, the West Indies, and Central and South America as far south as Bolivia.","title":"Zamia"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The range of one species ("Z. integrifolia", extends into the contiguous United States, i.e. Georgia and Florida.","title":"Zamia"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Sisymbrium altissimum is a species of "Sisymbrium".","title":"Sisymbrium altissimum"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The plant is native to the western part of the Mediterranean Basin in Europe and Northern Africa and is widely naturalized throughout most of the world, including all of North America.","title":"Sisymbrium altissimum"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It was probably introduced into North America by a contaminant crop seed.","title":"Sisymbrium altissimum"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The plant grows in soils of all textures, even sand.","title":"Sisymbrium altissimum"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The plant germinates in winter or early spring.","title":"Sisymbrium altissimum"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The blooming time is lengthy, and after maturity the plant forms a tumbleweed.","title":"Sisymbrium altissimum"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Common names of the plant include Jim Hill mustard, after James J. Hill, a Canadian-American railroad magnate, tall tumblemustard, tall mustard, tumble mustard, tumbleweed mustard, tall sisymbrium, and tall hedge mustard.","title":"Sisymbrium altissimum"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Carex stricta is a species of sedge known by the common names upright sedge and tussock sedge.","title":"Carex stricta"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is grass-like and can be difficult to distinguish from other plants, because of its long, triangular, green stems.","title":"Carex stricta"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The plant grows in moist marshes, forests and alongside bodies of water.","title":"Carex stricta"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It grows up to 2 ft tall and 2 ft wide.","title":"Carex stricta"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" When the leaves die, they build on top of or around the living plant, making a "tussock".","title":"Carex stricta"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" It is one of the most common wetland sedges in eastern North America.","title":"Carex stricta"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Salvia divinorum (also known as sage of the diviners, ska maría pastora, seer's sage, yerba de la pastora and just salvia) is a psychoactive plant which can induce visions and other spiritual experiences. Its native habitat is in cloud forest in the isolated Sierra Mazateca of Oaxaca, Mexico, where it grows in shady and moist locations.","title":"Salvia divinorum"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The plant grows to over a meter high, has hollow square stems, large leaves, and occasional white flowers with violet calyxes.","title":"Salvia divinorum"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Botanists have not determined whether "Salvia divinorum" is a cultigen or a hybrid; native plants reproduce vegetatively, rarely producing viable seed.","title":"Salvia divinorum"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Delphinium staphisagria is a species of "Delphinium", or larkspur, of the family Ranunculaceae.","title":"Delphinium staphisagria"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" This plant is also known as Lice-Bane or Stavesacre.","title":"Delphinium staphisagria"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It is described botanically as a stoutly-stemmed, hairy biennial with large palmate leaves up to 6 inches (15 cm) across.","title":"Delphinium staphisagria"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The flowers are mauve-blue to blue, short-spurred, and up to 1 inch (2.5 cm) across, occurring in racemes.","title":"Delphinium staphisagria"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The plant grows to a height of 4–5 feet.","title":"Delphinium staphisagria"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" It grows throughout the Mediterranean.","title":"Delphinium staphisagria"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" The dark-colored, wrinkled seeds of "D. staphisagria" are characteristically quite large (~5x6 mm), and it is likely that the species name, which translates to "wild raisin" (classical Greek: σταφὶς ἀγρία) is based on their appearance.","title":"Delphinium staphisagria"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" This name-derivation seems to have been arrived at independently by a modern horticulturalist, David Bassett, who also gives a detailed account of his experiences in growing this species.","title":"Delphinium staphisagria"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" All parts of this plant are highly toxic and should not be ingested in any quantity.","title":"Delphinium staphisagria"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Zamia
